    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Identify the revision pattern.\n\nStep 1 — Representative pattern: valid parentheses using a stack.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction isValidParentheses(value: string): boolean {\n  const stack: string[] = [];\n  const matching = new Map([\n    [\")\", \"(\"],\n    [\"]\", \"[\"],\n    [\"}\", \"{\"],\n  ]);\n\n  for (const char of value) {\n    if (char === \"(\" || char === \"[\" || char === \"{\") {\n      stack.push(char);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const expected = matching.get(char);\n    if (expected !== undefined && stack.pop() !== expected) {\n      return false;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return stack.length === 0;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction isValidParenthesesUsingReduce(value: string): boolean {\n  const stack: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const char of value) {\n    if (\"([{\".includes(char)) stack.push(char);\n    else if (\n      (char === \")\" && stack.pop() !== \"(\") ||\n      (char === \"]\" && stack.pop() !== \"[\") ||\n      (char === \"}\" && stack.pop() !== \"{\")\n    ) {\n      return false;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return stack.length === 0;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n\"({[]})\" → true\n\"([)]\" → false\n\nComplexity:\nO(n) time and O(n) worst-case stack space.\n\nStep 5 — Interview takeaway:\nUse the pattern only when its assumptions and invariant hold; always explain complexity.",
